      "text": "To find the value of \\( f(3) \\), we start with the given functional equation:\n\\[ f(x) + 2f(1 - x) = 3x^2 \\]\n\nFirst, substitute \\( x = 3 \\) into the equation:\n\\[ f(3) + 2f(1 - 3) = 3 \\cdot 3^2 \\]\n\\[ f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27 \\tag{1} \\]\n\nNext, substitute \\( x = -2 \\) into the original equation:\n\\[ f(-2) + 2f(1 - (-2)) = 3(-2)^2 \\]\n\\[ f(-2) + 2f(3) = 12 \\tag{2} \\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\( f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27 \\)\n2. \\( f(-2) + 2f(3) = 12 \\)\n\nTo eliminate \\( f(-2) \\), we can isolate \\( f(-2) \\) in equation (2):\n\\[ f(-2) = 12 - 2f(3) \\tag{3} \\]\n\nSubstitute equation (3) into equation (1):\n\\[ f(3) + 2(12 - 2f(3)) = 27 \\]\n\\[ f(3) + 24 - 4f(3) = 27 \\]\n\\[ -3f(3) + 24 = 27 \\]\n\\[ -3f(3) = 3 \\]\n\\[ f(3) = -1 \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( f(3) \\) is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).\n\nLet's confirm our result using Python to ensure the calculations are accurate.\n```python\n# Let's define the equations and solve for f(3)\n# We know that f(3) + 2*f(-2) = 27 and f(-2) + 2*f(3) = 12\n\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\n# Define the variables\nf3, f_neg2 = symbols('f3 f_neg2')\n\n# Define the equations\neq1 = Eq(f3 + 2*f_neg2, 27)\neq2 = Eq(f_neg2 + 2*f3, 12)\n\n# Solve the system of equations\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2), (f3, f_neg2))\nprint(solution)\n```",
